    "available_at": "2026-11-13T17:30:15.050632+00:00",
    "body": "The Pinto Valley site in Arizona. Credit: Capstone Copper.\nPosted By: FREDERIC TOMESCO November 13, 2026\nCapstone Copper (TSX: CS; ASX: CSC) has lowered the bottom of its annual copper production forecast by 5,000 tonnes after delaying the full restart of its Pinto Valley operation in Arizona until mid-December.\nThe primary crusher needs additional repairs to major mechanical components being manufactured off-site by the original equipment maker, the company said Thursday in an update . Mobile crushers should allow partial processing to resume in mid-November.\nWhile the 5,000-tonne impact is “modest,” it “adds to a difficult operational year at Pinto Valley and [the company’s] Mantoverde” operation in Chile, Jefferies mining analyst Tariq Fahad said Thursday in a note. “In our view, the bigger concern is execution, with investors likely looking for improved operating reliability into 2027.”\nCapstone shares dropped 4.5% to C$10.26 late Friday morning in Toronto, extending Thursday’s 6.5% decline. The company’s market capitalization now stands at about $7.9 billion (US$5.6 billion).\nAdditional pressure\nThe setback puts more pressure on a year-end recovery that Capstone was already counting on. In its Oct. 29 results, the company forecast annual production at the bottom of its 200,000-to-230,000-tonne range, with cash costs at the upper end of guidance.\nThe revised production floor is 195,000 tonnes. Capstone estimated the additional downtime would reduce this year’s copper output by about 5,000 tonnes, depending on restart timing. Temporary mobile crushers could support throughput of up to 40,000 tonnes daily during the interim restart.\nThat would be about two-thirds of Pinto Valley’s nominal milling capacity. The open-pit operation, about 130 km east of Phoenix, has a 60,000-tonne-per-day concentrator and produces copper and molybdenum concentrates as well as copper cathode.\nSupply risk\nCapstone is also grappling with the effects of a national strike in Chile. Called by Chilean trucking groups and a civic movement to protest high fuel prices, the stoppage threatens concentrate and cathode shipments and deliveries of fuel and sulphuric acid, Capstone said Thursday.\nPreventive measures and on-site storage have so far protected output at Capstone’s two Chilean operations, the company added. Sulphuric acid is used to leach copper from ore, while fuel powers mining equipment.\nTogether with the delays at Pinto Valley, the Chilean truckers’ strike introduces “incremental downside risk to year-end production,” TD Securities mining analyst Craig Hutchison said in a note.\nAt National Bank Financial, mining analyst Shane Nagle cut his rating on Capstone to sector perform from outperform, citing the “near-term operational uncertainty.”\nConsistent operational performance at Mantoverde and Pinto Valley “will be important to supporting a re-rating which has been materially impacted by [Thursday’s] release,” he said.\nHigher grades\nPinto Valley produced 6,358 tonnes of copper in the third quarter, compared with 9,949 tonnes a year earlier. Its nine-month output was 27,115 tonnes, down from 30,960 tonnes, Capstone reported in October.\nThe plant had restarted with mobile crushers on Oct. 1 before primary crusher commissioning began Oct. 21. Additional repairs were then identified.\nCapstone was looking to higher grades at Mantoverde and Mantos Blancos, alongside completion of the Mantoverde concentrator expansion tie-in, to lift fourth-quarter production. Group output totalled 45,218 tonnes in the third quarter, down 18% from a year earlier.\nThe company agreed in September to sell its Cozamin mine in Mexico to Luca Mining (TSXV: LUCA) for up to $385 million (C$549 million). Closing was expected in the fourth quarter, subject to conditions and approvals, Capstone said in October. The sale would add financial flexibility for the company’s growth projects.\nCapstone’s annual production target includes a full year of Cozamin output.\nPrint\nTAG: Arizona mining Chile copper copper production\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Canada South America United States\nCOMPANY: Capstone Copper\nRepublish this article",
